<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heinrich Heine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a>–<a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Harry Heine, Christian Johann Heinrich Heine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heinrich Heine was a German poet, writer and critic, born Harry Heine into a Jewish family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>, who converted to Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 1825. His early lyric poetry, collected in the Buch der Lieder of 1827, was set to music by Schubert, Mendelssohn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-schumann">Robert Schumann</a> and others; his later verse and prose are marked by satire and irony. Associated with the Young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> movement, he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1831, and in 1835 the German Diet banned his works with theirs; bedridden from 1848, he died in Paris in 1856. Nazi Germany burned and later banned his books, and a line from his play Almansor on burning books and people is engraved at the site of the 1933 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> book burning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-heine.jpg" alt="Heinrich Heine" width="330" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moritz Daniel Oppenheim · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_Heine-Oppenheim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…recorded four theories: that Luther and his companions sang it entering Worms for the Diet in April 1521 (<b>Heinrich Heine</b>); that it honoured Luther's friend Leonhard Kaiser, executed on 16 August 1527 (K. F. T. Schneider); that the Lutheran princes sang it entering Augsburg for the Diet of…</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-mighty-fortress-is-our-god">A Mighty Fortress Is Our God</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1824 <b>Heinrich Heine</b> took up Brentano's theme in &quot;Die Lorelei&quot;, one of his most famous poems.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lorelei">Die Lorelei</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.23,6.78&amp;year=1835" aria-label="Düsseldorf on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4704 389 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Heinrich Heine"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5154 659h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5154 659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.23,6.78&amp;year=1835">What was here in 1835? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The thought goes before the deed, as the lightning precedes the thunder.&#8221;</p><footer>Religion and Philosophy in Germany (1834) &#183; tr. S. L. Fleishman, revised, 1887 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37478/pg37478-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A drama will be enacted in Germany in comparison with which the French Revolution will appear a harmless idyl.&#8221;</p><footer>Religion and Philosophy in Germany (1834) &#183; tr. S. L. Fleishman, revised, 1887 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37478/pg37478-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…freedom is a new religion, the religion of our age. If Christ is not the God of this religion, he is still one of its high-priests, and his name shines consolingly in the hearts of its children.&#8221;</p><footer>English Fragments (1828), The Liberation &#183; tr. Charles Godfrey Leland, revised, 1887 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37478/pg37478-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Paris is the New Jerusalem, and the Rhine is the Jordan which separates the land of Freedom from the land of the Philistines.&#8221;</p><footer>English Fragments (1828), The Liberation &#183; tr. Charles Godfrey Leland, revised, 1887 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/37478/pg37478-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
